\DOC X86_CASEWISE_SUBROUTINE_SIM_ABBREV_TAC

\TYPE {X86_CASEWISE_SUBROUTINE_SIM_ABBREV_TAC : thm * (thm * (thm option array)) * int * thm * thm -> term list -> term -> string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Simulates a call to an inlined subroutine in an {eventually}-style x86 goal,
advancing the symbolic state variable past the call and abbreviating the
subroutine's output as a fresh variable constrained by a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_CASEWISE_SUBROUTINE_SIM_ABBREV_TAC} takes a tuple {(mc,ex,d,smc,cth)}
consisting of the caller's machine-code theorem, its execution/length theorem,
an integer offset of the callee's entry point inside the caller, the callee's
machine-code theorem and the callee's correctness theorem; a list {ins} of
terms describing the subroutine's inputs as read from the pre-state (registers,
memory contents, return address, stack pointer); a term {outs} describing the
subroutine's output as read from the post-state {s'}; a string {s} used as
prefix for the name of the new state variable; and an integer {n} appended to
that prefix to form the new state name.

It yields a single subgoal whose conclusion has the same {eventually} body,
with the state argument replaced by the freshly named successor state. Every
assumption mentioning the old state is discharged and replaced by the
corresponding assumption about the new state: program-counter and stack-pointer
readings at the return site, the loaded-code fact, saved stack slots, unchanged
memory contents, and an updated accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the
initial state to the new state, extended with the components the subroutine may
write. Flag assumptions about the old state are simply dropped, and assumptions
not mentioning the state are left untouched. Two assumptions are added: an
equation defining a fresh variable for the subroutine's result, as given by the
correctness theorem and possibly guarded by a side condition, and an equation
stating that the output location in the new state reads as that fresh variable.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour on goals
of the same {eventually} shape.

\ENDDOC
